\section{Event Logger (Phase 7.4)}

The [[EventLogger]] instruments memory writes to capture events scoped to
subroutine invocations. It is flag-gated: when disabled, no overhead is
incurred beyond the flag check.

Subroutine invocation detection works by checking the Z80 program counter
against the known entry points on each instruction step. When PC matches
an entry point, a new [[InvocationRecord]] is opened. Events from memory
writes are tagged with the current invocation.

<<event logger>>=
class EventLogger {
public:
    EventLogger() = default;

    void set_enabled(bool e) { m_enabled = e; }
    bool enabled() const { return m_enabled; }

    // Called before each instruction to detect subroutine entry.
    void check_pc(uint16_t pc, uint16_t sp, bool interrupt_pending);

    // Record a memory write event.
    void record_write(EventType type, EventRegion region,
                      uint16_t addr, uint8_t old_val, uint8_t new_val);

    // Record a collision flag change.
    void record_collision(uint8_t old_val, uint8_t new_val);

    // At frame boundary: finalize the frame log and return it.
    FrameLog finalize_frame(int frame_number,
                            const uint8_t* vram, uint8_t intercept);

    // Set the entry point table (non-owning pointer).
    void set_entry_points(const SubroutineEntryPoints* ep) { m_entry_points = ep; }

private:
    bool m_enabled = false;
    const SubroutineEntryPoints* m_entry_points = nullptr;

    // Per-address invocation counters.
    std::unordered_map<uint16_t, uint32_t> m_invocation_counts;

    // Current frame's invocation records.
    std::vector<InvocationRecord> m_invocations;

    // Whether the next subroutine entry should be tagged as interrupt-driven.
    bool m_interrupt_pending = false;
};
@

